f any offence", "Code": "C167", "Answer": "A"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Trial Process", "Question": "Karen is a security guard at a bank. During a robbery, she is attacked and injured. Without thinking, Karen immediately reacts by firing her gun, killing the thief. She is charged with murder. What defence would be appropriate at her trial?", "A": "Automatism", "B": "Duress", "C": "Mental illness", "D": "Neccessity", "Code": "C168", "Answer": "A"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Trial Process", "Question": "Helen was charged charged with murder. Her defenced of diminished responsibility was accepted by the court and Helen was found guilty of manslaughter. For Helen’s defence to be accepted by the court it was necessary for her to show evidence that she:", "A": "Was provoked", "B": "Was under duress", "C": "Suffers from a mental abnormality that caused her to commit the crime", "D": "Acted in self defence", "Code": "C169", "Answer": "C"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "George is being prosecuted for a robbery in NSW. George gives evidence that he was in Victoria at the time of the offence. Which of the following does this evidence challenge?", "A": "Mens rea", "B": "Causation", "C": "Actus reus", "D": "Doli incapax", "Code": "C17", "Answer": "C"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Trial Process", "Question": "An accused at his hearing that he was provoked when he assaulted the victim. How is this evidence relevant?", "A": "The magistrate can reduce the charge.", "B": "The evidence of provocation is only relevant in sentencing.", "C": "The defence must prove the victim provoked the accused.", "D": "If successful, the evidence of provocation will lead to an acquittal.", "Code": "C170", "Answer": "B"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Trial Process", "Question": "An accused is on trial for armed robbery and claims that the crime was committed under the threat of death. What would be the most likely defence?", "A": "Duress", "B": "Consent", "C": "Provocation", "D": "Automatism", "Code": "C171", "Answer": "A"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Trial Process", "Question": "What is the main role of a jury in a criminal trial?", "A": "To determine the penalty", "B": "To represent the views of victims", "C": "To decide if the accused person is guilty", "D": "To advise the judge on community standards", "Code": "C172", "Answer": "C"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Trial Process", "Question": "What is the role of the jury in a criminal trial?", "A": "To provide reasons for their decision in the case", "B": "To decide on an appropriate sentence for the accused", "C": "To find evidence to determine the verdict of the case", "D": "To listen to the evidence and then hand down their verdict", "Code": "C173", "Answer": "D"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Trial Process", "Question": "Who determines if the standard of proof has been met in a criminal case?", "A": "The jury", "B": "The police", "C": "The defendant", "D": "The prosecution", "Code": "C174", "Answer": "A"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Trial Process", "Question": "Which of the following statements is true about a jury’s verdict?", "A": "A jury must return a unanimous verdict", "B": "Only 10 of the 12 jurors have to agree with the verdict", "C": "A jury can return a majority verdict after 8 hours of deliberation", "D": "A judge must direct that a majority verdict is possible before the jury retires", "Code": "C175", "Answer": "C"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Trial Process", "Question": "Of the following statements, which is TRUE in regards to unanimous verdicts in NSW?", "A": "Majority verdicts are allowed where it is unlikely the jury will reach a unanimous verdict after 8 hours", "B": "Unanimous verdicts are to be used for all serious indictable offences", "C": "Majority verdicts are allowed when a juror becomes ill during the trial and the judges orders", "D": "Unanimous verdicts are not used in NSW anymore", "Code": "C176", "Answer": "A"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Trial Process", "Question": "The Supreme Court is hearing a criminal case. At the conclusion of the third day, two jurors visit the crime scene. They discuss the defendant's evidence and then encourage other jurors to find the defendant guilty. What is this example of?", "A": "A role of the jury", "B": "A denial of evidence", "C": "An aggravating factor", "D": "A peremptory challenge", "Code": "C177", "Answer": "B"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Trial Process", "Question": "Which of the following is the best example of a jury making a majority verdict?", "A": "A verdict where all jurors must agree, such as 12-0", "B": "A verdict when only one juror may object, such as 11-1", "C": "A verdict where a significant number of jurors must agree, such as 10-2", "D": "A verdict where most jurors agree, such as 7-5", "Code": "C178", "Answer": "B"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Trial Process", "Question": "In a jury trial, a peremptory challenge refers to a situation where", "A": "A juror is struck out because they may be considered prejudicial to the case", "B": "A juror is struck out without a specific reason given", "C": "A juror is strick out because there is evidence of trial by media", "D": "A juror is struck out because they work for emergency services", "Code": "C179", "Answer": "B"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "Michael is charged with sexual assault after his DNA was found on the victim’s jacket. A witness testifies that Michael was at work during the time of the crime. Using this evidence, which element of the crime is Michael disputing?", "A": "Strict liability offence", "B": "Causation", "C": "Mens rea", "D": "Actus reus", "Code": "C18", "Answer": "D"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Trial Process", "Question": "The 2006 Jury Amendments (Verdict) Act (NSW) allowed for majority verdicts in NSW trials. Under which of the following circumstances would majority verdicts be permissible?", "A": "When 9 out of 12 jurors agree on a verdict", "B": "When there is a hung jury", "C": "Where reasonable time for deliberation has occurred and the court is satisfied a unanimous verdict can’t be reached", "D": "In all Commonwealth criminal trials", "Code": "C180", "Answer": "C"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Trial Process", "Question": "Which of the following can be used as a partial defence to murder in NSW?", "A": "Consent", "B": "Mental Illness", "C": "Automatism", "D": "Provocation", "Code": "C181", "Answer": "D"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Trial Process", "Question": "Ivy is arrested. She remains silent during police questioning and is charged with the murder of George. Before the trial, Ivy gives her lawyer new evidence that she was pressured to kill George by a voice in her head. Which statement is true?", "A": "Ivy can raise duress as a partial defence to murder", "B": "Ivy cannot introduce new evidence in the trial", "C": "Ivy may receive a sentencing discount if she pleads guilty before the trial", "D": "Ivy can raise mental health or cognitive impairment as a partial defence to murder", "Code": "C182", "Answer": "C"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Trial Process", "Question": "Which of the following is a complete defence to an offence against the person?", "A": "Provocation", "B": "Excessive self defence", "C": "Substantial impairment by abnormality of the mind", "D": "Mental health impairment or cognitive impairment", "Code": "C183", "Answer": "D"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Trial Process", "Question": "Elizabeth, who is a former Supreme Court judge, stands trial for murder in NSW. The jury returns a special verdict of “act proven but not criminally responsible.” What will most likely happen to Elizabeth next?", "A": "She will be sentenced", "B": "She will be placed in protective custody", "C": "She will be released to the community", "D": "She will be assessed by the Mental Health Review Tribunal", "Code": "C184", "Answer": "D"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Trial Process", "Question": "During trial, Kourtney claims she was acting unlawfully as she was trying to prevent a more serious consequence from occurring. What type of defence is Kourtney attempting to use?", "A": "Duress", "B": "Necesity", "C": "Automatism", "D": "Mental Illness", "Code": "C185", "Answer": "B"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Trial Process", "Question": "To successfully use a defence of involuntary behaviour, what needs to be proved?", "A": "The accused committed the crime against their own free will.", "B": "The accused actions could not be controlled.", "C": "The accused carried out the crime in the act of defending themselves or another person.", "D": "The accused suffers from a mental abnormality that caused them to act in a certain manner and carry out the crime.", "Code": "C186", "Answer": "B"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Trial Process", "Question": "Remi raises evidence at his trial for manslaughter that he has a mental illness. How would this defence be dealt with in court?", "A": "Remi must prove it on the balance of probabilities", "B": "The evidence can only be used if the provocation agrees", "C": "The judge must give the jury a warning that the evidence may not be reliable", "D": "The prosecution would only have to prove the mens rea on the balance of probabilites", "Code": "C187", "Answer": "A"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Trial Process", "Question": "Which of the following accurately describes the role of a judge and jury in a criminal trial?", "A": "Judge determines sentence; jury ensures accused has legal aid", "B": "Judge decides if standard of proof is met; jury determines sentence", "C": "Judge determines what evidence is admissible; jury decides if standard of proof is met", "D": "Judge directs the jury; jury allocates burden of proof", "Code": "C188", "Answer": "C"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Trial Process", "Question": "Glenn is aged 45 and enters a guilty plea to a manslaughter charge two days before his trial is due to commence. How would this guilty plea affect Glenn’s sentence?", "A": "The Prosecutor can now decide Glenn’s sentence", "B": "The Judge must give a discount to Glenn’s sentence", "C": "The Judge has discretion to reduce Glenn’s sentence", "D": "The plea is too late and so it won’t affect Glenn’s sentence", "Code": "C189", "Answer": "B"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "Riley had been drinking and, while being driven home, was swearing at people out of the car window. The driver was stopped by police and Riley was issued a fine for using offensive language. Into which category of crime does this offence fall?", "A": "Drug", "B": "Driving", "C": "Economic", "D": "Public order", "Code": "C19", "Answer": "D"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Trial Process", "Question": "Under the Crimes Act 1900 (NSW), which of the following is a legal defence to murder?", "A": "Self defence", "B": "Lack of legal representation", "C": "The opinion that the law is unfair", "D": "Being aware that the conduct is illegal", "Code": "C190", "Answer": "A"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Trial Process", "Question": "Beau is charged with aggravated sexual assault. Due to the complexity of the case and significant media coverage, Beau requests a trial by judge alone. The court approves, and the trial proceeds without a jury. Which of the following is a key feature of this type of trial?", "A": "Beau will have to prove his innocence", "B": "The verdict is determined by a panel of judges", "C": "The judge will provide reasons following the verdict", "D": "The sentence is determined before the verdict is delivered", "Code": "C191", "Answer": "C"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Trial Process", "Question": "During a criminal trial, a court discovers that some evidence has been obtained unlawfully. How should this evidence be treated?", "A": "It should be deemed inadmissible.", "B": "It should be excluded at the discretion of the judge.", "C": "It can be used in the case of a minor offence.", "D": "It should be admitted if it is relevant to the case.", "Code": "C192", "Answer": "B"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Trial Process", "Question": "Jackson is charged with murder. Which scenario would result in the application of a complete defence under NSW criminal law?", "A": "Claiming that he was provoked during an argument and reacted violently", "B": "Claiming that he was forced to commit the act under threat of harm to his family", "C": "Claiming that he had consumed alcohol before committing the offence", "D": "Claiming that he used reasonable force to defend himself from a life-threatening attack", "Code": "C193", "Answer": "D"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Trial Process", "Question": "A jury has found Rujoola guilty of committing a burglary. How might the court use a victim impact statement during her sentencing?", "A": "To determine the final sentence", "B": "To understand the impact of the crime on the victim", "C": "To formally assess guilt in relation to acts reus", "D": "As a crucial piece of evidence if the jury’s decision is unanimous", "Code": "C194", "Answer": "B"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Trial Process", "Question": "An accused is found guilty of manslaughter. During sentencing, the victim’s family reads a statement to the court describing how the incident has affected their daily lives. Which of the following best describes this process?", "A": "Freedom of speech", "B": "A victim impact statement", "C": "The right of the community", "D": "Mitigating factors affecting sentencing", "Code": "C195", "Answer": "B"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Trial Process", "Question": "What is the role of a jury in a criminal trial?", "A": "Decide the guilt of the alleged offender", "B": "Decide the punishment for the offender", "C": "Assist in the trial by asking the offender questions", "D": "Decide if victim impact statements should be allowed", "Code": "C196", "Answer": "A"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Trial Process", "Question": "Which of the following is a role of the foreperson of the jury in a criminal trial?", "A": "To deliver the offender’s sentence on the jury’s behalf", "B": "To deliver the verdict on the jury’s behalf", "C": "To research the facts of the case on the jury’s behalf", "D": "To ensure the jury reaches a unanimous verdict", "Code": "C197", "Answer": "B"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Trial Process", "Question": "What is the purpose of cross examination of a witness in a criminal trial?", "A": "To allow the judge to ask questions", "B": "To allow for a victim impact statement", "C": "To test the evidence given by the witness", "D": "To allow the witness to explain their evidence to the jury", "Code": "C198", "Answer": "C"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Trial Process", "Question": "Why may Legal Aid NOT be granted for a criminal case?", "A": "The Director of Public Prosecutions declines the accused’s request.", "B": "The police determine the offender doesn’t qualify for Legal Aid.", "C": "The accused does not meet the means test requirements.", "D": "The matter is an indictable offence.", "Code": "C199", "Answer": "C"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "An employee of a bank becomes aware of confidential information which will increase the bank’s share price. Before the information is publicly released, the employee buys shared in the bank. What is this an example of?", "A": "A conspiracy", "B": "A preliminary offence", "C": "A public order offence", "D": "An economic offence", "Code": "C20", "Answer": "D"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Trial Process", "Question": "Who makes the findings of fact in a District Court Case?", "A": "Jury", "B": "Defence", "C": "Magistrate", "D": "Prosecution", "Code": "C200", "Answer": "A"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Trial Process", "Question": "Rachel is on trial for committing physical assault against another person. She claims that she was experiencing severe emotional distress that impaired her judgement at the time of the assault. What defence is Rachel using?", "A": "duress", "B": "necessity", "C": "provocation", "D": "automatism", "Code": "C201", "Answer": "D"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Trial Process", "Question": "What are the 2 primary aims of the Jury Amendment (Verdicts) Act 2006 (NSW)?", "A": "To reduce the occurrence of hung juries and to increase imprisonment rates", "B": "To protect witnesses and to decrease the role of the judge in guiding juries", "C": "To reduce the need for judge-alone trials and to support the appeals process", "D": "To increase the number of successful convictions and to improve the efficiency of courts", "Code": "C202", "Answer": "D"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Trial Process", "Question": "Ian has pleaded guilty to murder and has subsequently received a reduced prison sentence. What is the term used to describe this process?", "A": "Suspended sentence", "B": "Charge negotiation", "C": "Non-parole period", "D": "Intensive correction order", "Code": "C203", "Answer": "B"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Trial Process", "Question": "A criminal case is heard in the Supreme Court. Which option most accurately shows the outcome if the given event occurred?", "A": "Event: Misconduct by the prosecution. Outcome: Charges dismissed.", "B": "Event: A hung jury. Outcome: Not guilty verdict delivered.", "C": "Event: Irrelevant witness testimonies. Outcome: Defendant freed.", "D": "Event: Evidence tampering. Outcome: Retrial of case.", "Code": "C204", "Answer": "D"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Trial Process", "Question": "In a criminal trial, what is the role of the judge?", "A": "Determine the innocence of an alleged offender", "B": "Determine the guilt of an alleged offender", "C": "Ensure rules of evidence are followed", "D": "To check all evidence presented", "Code": "C205", "Answer": "C"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Trial Process", "Question": "Which of the following statements is true regarding a criminal trial in the NSW Supreme Court", "A": "The plaintiff has the burden to prove the defendant’s guilt beyond reasonable doubt.", "B": "The prosecution has the burden to prove the defendant’s guilt beyond reasonable doubt.", "C": "The plaintiff has the burden to prove the defendant’s guilt on the balance of probabilities.", "D": "The prosecution has the burden to prove the defendant’s guilt on the balance of probabilities.", "Code": "C206", "Answer": "B"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Trial Process", "Question": "In NSW, what is the primary legal purpose of the provocation defence?", "A": "To excuse criminal liability", "B": "To mitigate the severity of the charge and punishment", "C": "To shift the burden of proof in a criminal trial to the defence", "D": "To establish a complete defence for a criminal act prior to sentencing", "Code": "C207", "Answer": "B"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Trial Process", "Question": "Which of the following challenges the presumption of innocence?", "A": "Bail", "B": "Remand", "C": "The right to remain silent", "D": "The principle of double jeopardy", "Code": "C208", "Answer": "B"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Trial Process", "Question": "Which of the following is correct about a person’s right to legal aid, when on trial for a serious indictable offence?", "A": "All accused persons have the right to legal aid in NSW.", "B": "Defendants have the right to legal aid, only if they plead guilty.", "C": "The right to legal aid is afforded when there is an injustice likely to occur.", "D": "Legal aid is only available to cases that are heard in the Supreme Court of NSW.", "Code": "C209", "Answer": "C"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "Kim has damaged a number of garden gnomes in houses in a local neighbourhood garden. What type of offence has Kim committed?", "A": "A property offence", "B": "An indictable offence", "C": "A public order offence", "D": "A strict liability offence", "Code": "C21", "Answer": "A"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Trial Process", "Question": "David was forced at gunpoint to commit a crime. Which complete defence could David use to justify his actions?", "A": "Duress", "B": "Automatism", "C": "Provocation", "D": "Mental illness", "Code": "C210", "Answer": "A"}, {"Syllabus point": "Sentencing and Punishment", "Question": "Which of the following is a purpose of punishment?", "A": "Interrogation", "B": "Imprisonment", "C": "Rehabilitation", "D": "Restorative Justice", "Code": "C210", "Answer": "C"}, {"Syllabus point": "Sentencing and Punishment", "Question": "In sentencing an offender, the judge focuses on the importance of retribution. What is the judge’s aim in focusing on this?", "A": "To penalise the offender", "B": "To reduce future criminal behaviour", "C": "To give the offender employment skills", "D": "To educate the community about criminal behaviour", "Code": "C211", "Answer": "A"}, {"Syllabus point": "Sentencing and Punishment", "Question": "The purpose of specific deterrence in sentencing is to", "A": "Direct the offender to undertake education and training", "B": "Direct the offender to complete a community service order", "C": "Discourage others who may consider committing a similar offence", "D": "Discourage the offender from committing the offence again", "Code": "C212", "Answer": "D"}, {"Syllabus point": "Sentencing and Punishment", "Question": "Which of the following is NOT a purpose of punishment?", "A": "Incapacitation", "B": "Rehabilitation", "C": "Deterrence", "D": "Recidivism", "Code": "C213", "Answer": "D"}, {"Syllabus point": "Sentencing and Punishment", "Question": "Which of the following is a purpose of punishment?", "A": "Community service", "B": "Periodic detention", "C": "Imprisonment", "D": "Retribution", "Code": "C214", "Answer": "D"}, {"Syllabus point": "Sentencing and Punishment", "Question": "A person has been found guilty of aggravated assault. The judge has heard from the victim as well as referring to similar court cases prior to sentencing. Why has the judge referred to similar cases?", "A": "To follow judicial guidelines", 