uce the jurisdiction of the Children’s Court", "B": "To increase the level of recidivism for young offenders", "C": "To increase the severity of offences for young offenders", "D": "To decrease the level of recidivism for young offenders", "Code": "C298", "Answer": "D"}, {"Syllabus point": "Young Offenders", "Question": "19-year-old Agatha is facing court on a charge of common assault. Can the Children’s court hear this matter?", "A": "No, Agatha is no longer considered a child", "B": "No, unless she pleads guilty", "C": "Yes, if it is less than 6 months since the time of the offence", "D": "Yes, if Agatha was under 18 at the time of the offence", "Code": "C299", "Answer": "D"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "Michael travels by public transport to his office in the city. He has been caught travelling without a valid ticket. What type of offences have been committed by Michael?", "A": "Summary and public order", "B": "Summary and strict liability", "C": "White collar and public order", "D": "White collar and strict liability", "Code": "C30", "Answer": "B"}, {"Syllabus point": "Young Offenders", "Question": "Brimsley is 12 years old and is charged with malicious damage. Which of the following applies in this case?", "A": "Bromley cannot be charged because of doli incapax.", "B": "The prosecution must rebut the presumption of doli incapax.", "C": "The defence must prove on the balance of probabilities that doli incapax applies.", "D": "The defence must prove beyond reasonable doubt that doli incapax applies.", "Code": "C300", "Answer": "B"}, {"Syllabus point": "Young Offenders", "Question": "The rights of victims of youth crime are best acknowledged by", "A": "The Children’s Court", "B": "Youth justice conferencing", "C": "The application of doli incapax", "D": "The principles outlined in the Children (Criminal Proceedings) Act 1987", "Code": "C301", "Answer": "B"}, {"Syllabus point": "Young Offenders", "Question": "Chantal is 16 years old and is ca