ainst the international community?", "A": "War crimes and murder", "B": "War crimes and genocide", "C": "Genocide and tax evasion", "D": "People smuggling and genocide", "Code": "C331", "Answer": "B"}, {"Syllabus point": "International Crime", "Question": "A person has committed a serious indictable offence and has served a 5-year prison sentence. Upon being released from prison the Minister of Immigration forces this individual to leave the country. This is known as", "A": "deportation.", "B": "expulsion.", "C": "extradition.", "D": "repatriation.", "Code": "C332", "Answer": "A"}, {"Syllabus point": "International Crime", "Question": "Stewart is a citizen of Brazil and has been living in Australia on a tourist visa. He is accused of committing a serious indictable offence in Australia. Which legal instrument would govern the process of returning Stewart to Brazil?", "A": "An order from the High Court of Australia", "B": "A bilateral agreement between Australia and Brazil", "C": "A multilateral agreement between Australia and Brazil", "D": "A deportation notice from the Attorney-General of Australia", "Code": "C333", "Answer": "B"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "John assists Shaun in hiding the knife with which Shaun has just stabbed someone. Which of the following best describes John?", "A": "An accessory before the fact", "B": "A principal in the first degree", "C": "A principal in the second degree", "D": "An accessory after the fact", "Code": "C34", "Answer": "D"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "Mary was found to be an accessory before the fact in a bank robbery. Which of the following would have led to this finding?", "A": "Mary supplied the gun that was used.", "B": "Mary used the gun at the crime scene.", "C": "Mary carried the money out of the bank.", "D": "Mary hid the money taken from the bank.", "Code": "C35", "Answer": "A"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "Sam helped Chris plan a crime. Chris drove to Sam’s house on his way to a bank. Sam gave Chris a weapon. Sam then stayed at home. Chris used the weapon to rob the bank. What is Sam likely to be charged with?", "A": "Accessory after the fact", "B": "Accessory before the fact", "C": "Principal in the first degree", "D": "Principal in the second degree", "Code": "C36", "Answer": "B"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "Patricia murders her friend Joan. That evening, Patricia seeks refuge at her brother Donald’s house. Which statement best describes Donald’s involvement in the murder?", "A": "Principal in the first degree", "B": "Principal in the second degree", "C": "Accessory before the fact", "D": "Accessory after the fact", "Code": "C37", "Answer": "D"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "Sabine, Jinbao and Matthew plan to rob a bank. Renata is an employee of the bank and provides them with details of how to access the bank’s vault. On the day of the robbery, Sabine enters the bank and steals money from the bank’s vault and Matthew drives the getaway car. What is Renata’s role in the crime?", "A": "Accessory after the fact", "B": "Accessory before the fact", "C": "Principal in the first degree", "D": "Principal in the second degree", "Code": "C38", "Answer": "B"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "Frank is unemployed and homeless. He is caught travelling on a train without a valid ticket. He uses offensive language and assaults a police officer in an effort to escape. He is arrested and charged with a number of offences. Frank plans to plead not guilty. Which factor affecting criminal behaviour is most likely to be causing Frank’s actions?", "A": "Social and economic factors", "B": "Genetic factors", "C": "Political factors", "D": "Self interest", "Code": "C39", "Answer": "A"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "Katherine has decided to protest for action against climate change by spraying graffiti on the wall of her local train station. Which factor affecting criminal behaviour is most likely to be causing her actions?", "A": "Social and economic factors", "B": "Genetic factors", "C": "Political factors", "D": "Self interest", "Code": "C40", "Answer": "C"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "Pedro is arrested by the police after he is identified on CCTV stealing groceries from his local Colesworth supermarket. During questioning he tells the police that he stole the items for his family as he had recently become unemployed and could not afford to buy them. Which of the following factors is most likely causing Pedro’s criminal behaviour?", "A": "Genetic factors", "B": "Political factors", "C": "Social and economic factors", "D": "Self interest", "Code": "C41", "Answer": "C"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "Which of the following factors is likely to affect criminal behaviour?", "A": "Drug abuse", "B": "Homelessness", "C": "Unemployment", "D": "All of the above", "Code": "C42", "Answer": "D"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "Kim and Nick plan to import drugs into Australia and sell them at local shopping centres. Their motivation for this criminal behaviour is best classified as:", "A": "Genetic", "B": "Political", "C": "Self interest", "D": "Economic", "Code": "C43", "Answer": "C"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "As a result of crowd violence leading to arrests at previous games, fans from opposing teams are now required to sit on different sides of the playing field. What is this an example of?", "A": "A diversionary program", "B": "An exercise of police discretion", "C": "A social crime prevention strategy", "D": "A situational crime prevention strategy", "Code": "C44", "Answer": "D"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "Which of the following is an example of situational crime prevention?", "A": "A youth drop-in centre", "B": "Job training for the unemployed", "C": "A drug and alcohol education program", "D": "Drug-detection dogs at a music festival", "Code": "C45", "Answer": "D"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "A local council is considering the installation of closed circuit television (CCTV) in a park to deter criminal activity. What is this an example of?", "A": "Law enforcement", "B": "Situational policing", "C": "Crime prevention", "D": "Situational crime prevention", "Code": "C46", "Answer": "D"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "Which of the following is an example of situational crime prevention?", "A": "Literacy programs for disadvantage children", "B": "Increased funding for on the job skills training", "C": "Drug and alcohol rehabilitation", "D": "Encryption of computer data", "Code": "C47", "Answer": "D"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "The government expands education and skills-based training within prisons. What is this an example of?", "A": "Situational crime prevention", "B": "Social crime prevention", "C": "Diversionary programs", "D": "Restorative Justice", "Code": "C48", "Answer": "B"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "Matthew is caught speeding and is issued with a fine. Which of the following best describes this offence?", "A": "Indictable", "B": "Accessory", "C": "Strict liability", "D": "Second degree", "Code": "C49", "Answer": "C"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "At the conclusion of a trial, Johannes is found not guilty of murder, but guilty of manslaughter. Which of the following statements is true in relation to the murder charge?", "A": "Actus Reus was proved beyond reasonable doubt, but Mens Rea was not.", "B": "Mens Rea was proved beyond reasonable doubt, but Actus Reus was not.", "C": "Both Actus Reus and Mens Rea were proved beyond reasonable doubt.", "D": "Neither Actus Reus nor Mens Rea was proved beyond reasonable doubt.", "Code": "C50", "Answer": "A"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "A government introduces sexual consent classes for all Year 12 students. What type of crime prevention strategy is being used here?", "A": "Social", "B": "Situational", "C": "General deterrence", "D": "Specific deterrence", "Code": "C51", "Answer": "A"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "Which of the following offences would NOT be heard summarily?", "A": "Larceny", "B": "Speeding", "C": "Malicious damage", "D": "Armed robbery", "Code": "C52", "Answer": "D"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "Which of the following best distinguishes a summary offence from an indictable offence under NSW legislation?", "A": "Summary offences are always heard by a jury", "B": "Indictable offences cannot result in a criminal conviction", "C": "Indictable offences are dealt with by way of an on-the-spot fine", "D": "Summary offences are less serious and are usually heard in the Local Court", "Code": "C53", "Answer": "D"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "Leo and Mia agree to rob a jewellery store. They plan the details and purchase tools for the break-in, but are arrested by police before the robbery takes place. Both are charged with conspiracy to commit robbery under NSW law. Which of the following best explains why they can still be charged, even though robbery never occurred?", "A": "Because discussing illegal activity is always a criminal offence in NSW", "B": "Because an offence only applies if the planned crime actually occurred", "C": "Because an agreement has taken place to commit a crime even if the act does not occur", "D": "Because they bought tools that can be used in a robbery, which is a separate offence", "Code": "C54", "Answer": "C"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "After being caught consuming alcohol in a public place, Charlotte receives a Criminal Infringement Notice (CIN) from a NSW police officer. Which of the following best describes a feature of a CIN?", "A": "It cannot be disputed once issues", "B": "It requires Charlotte to appear in court", "C": "It results in a permanent criminal record", "D": "It provides a way to deal with minor offences without court proceedings", "Code": "C55", "Answer": "D"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "The outside walls of several buildings in Sydney have large art works painted on them. This is an example of", "A": "Social crime prevention", "B": "Situational crime prevention", "C": "A program to increase safety", "D": "Increased social acceptance of graffiti", "Code": "C56", "Answer": "B"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "In which court would a police prosecutor bring charges against an alleged offender?", "A": "Children’s", "B": "Coroner’s", "C": "District", "D": "Federal", "Code": "C57", "Answer": "A"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "Which of the following describes a diversionary program?", "A": "A form of punishment that includes incarceration", "B": "A measure aimed at diverting offenders away from the court system", "C": "A program offering automatic bail for non-violent offenders", "D": "A rehabilitation program for repeat offenders only", "Code": "C58", "Answer": "B"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "Which of the following distinguishes between summary and indictable offences?", "A": "Summary offences are heard before a jury, indictable offences are heard by a magistrate", "B": "Summary offences are punishable by imprisonment, indictable offences are punishable by fines only", "C": "Summary offences are heard in the Local Court, indictable offences are heard in higher courts", "D": "Summary offences involve police discretion, indictable offences do not involve police discretion", "Code": "C59", "Answer": "C"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "During a particular trial, the prosecution must rove that the accused physically carried out a prohibited action, regardless of intent. Which of the following is the prosecution seeking to establish?", "A": "mens rea", "B": "actus reus", "C": "Strict liability", "D": "Causation", "Code": "C60", "Answer": "B"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "In the case of Jones v R, which of the following is correct?", "A": "This represents a civil case matter", "B": "Jones is appealing a conviction or sentence", "C": "The burden of proof rests with the prosecution", "D": "New evidence must be brought forward in this case", "Code": "C61", "Answer": "B"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "Michelle has been caught making phone calls to local schools pretending to make bomb threats, causing emergency evacuations. Which type of offence does this best represent?", "A": "Economic offence", "B": "Preliminary offence", "C": "Public order offence", "D": "Crimes against the state", "Code": "C62", "Answer": "C"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "John and his mates are drinking in the local park and are asked to move on by a police officer. John loses his ‘cool’, refuses to move on and uses abusive and threatening language at the police officer. What type of offence is this an example of?", "A": "Preliminary offence", "B": "Public order offence", "C": "Offence against the person", "D": "Offence against the sovereign", "Code": "C63", "Answer": "B"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "Which of the following is an example of social crime prevention?", "A": "An accused being remanded in custody", "B": "A curfew banning young people from being out in public at night", "C": "Police drug-detection dogs at festivals", "D": "An employment program for young people", "Code": "C64", "Answer": "D"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "The NSW Government increases random breath testing operations during the Christmas holidays. This is best described as:", "A": "Sedition", "B": "Social crime prevention", "C": "A breach of the rule of law", "D": "Situational crime prevention", "Code": "C65", "Answer": "D"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "Which pair of aspects of the criminal justice system are NOT closely related?", "A": "Appeals and court jurisdiction", "B": "Bail and the unacceptable risk test", "C": "Young offenders and rehabilitation", "D": "Mandatory sentencing and judicial discretion", "Code": "C66", "Answer": "D"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "Stealing a person’s property is categorised as", "A": "An economic offence", "B": "A preliminary offence", "C": "An offence against the person", "D": "An offence against the community", "Code": "C67", "Answer": "A"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "A high school coordinates with guest speakers from a community centre and the local police to create a program designed to inform Year 12 students of the risks of drink driving. Which of the following best describes this?", "A": "Police crackdowns", "B": "Social crime prevention", "C": "Community service initiative", "D": "Situational crime prevention", "Code": "C68", "Answer": "B"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "Which court would a trial for murder be heard in?", "A": "The High Court", "B": "The Local Court", "C": "The District Court", "D": "The Supreme Court", "Code": "C69", "Answer": "D"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "Which of the following is considered a strict liability offence?", "A": "Attempted murder", "B": "Embezzlement of funds", "C": "Exceeding the speed limit in a school zone", "D": "Possession of a commercial quantity of drugs", "Code": "C70", "Answer": "C"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "A local council is looking to reduce youth crime in their local community. As a result, they have introduced a youth diversionary program and a parenting workshop. These are examples of", "A": "Juvenile rehabilitation", "B": "Preventative detention", "C": "Social crime prevention", "D": "Situational crime prevention", "Code": "C71", "Answer": "C"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "Maddison and Anna carry out the armed robbery of a convenience store, whilst Elena waits in the getaway car outside. Elena drives them back to Jeffrey’s house, who then hides the money. Who would be charged as principal in the second degree?", "A": "Anna", "B": "Elena", "C": "Jeffrey", "D": "Maddison", "Code": "C72", "Answer": "B"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "Which of the following is an example of a summary offence?", "A": "Murder", "B": "Kidnapping", "C": "Obstructing traffic", "D": "International drug trafficking", "Code": "C73", "Answer": "C"}, {"Syllabus point": "Nature of crime", "Question": "Which statement best describes a person who is the principal in the first degree to a crime?", "A": "They carried out the crime.", "B": "They helped plan the crime.", "C": "They are denied bail in all circumstances.", "D": "They assisted after the actual crime was committed.", "Code": "C74", "Answer": "A"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Investigation Process", "Question": "Under what circumstances do the police have the power to arrest a person?", "A": "Only when a court has issued an arrest warrant", "B": "When the police need to question a person suspected of committing a crime", "C": "When the police intend to take a person they suspect of committing a crime before a court", "D": "When the police need to search a person who they believe is carrying an illegal item", "Code": "C75", "Answer": "C"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Investigation Process", "Question": "When do the police have the power to stop, detain and search a person or their vehicle?", "A": "When the police have reasonable grounds to suspect the person is carrying an illegal item", "B": "When the person has a criminal record", "C": "Only when the police have obtained a search warrant", "D": "Only after the police have arrested the person", "Code": "C76", "Answer": "A"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Investigation Process", "Question": "Jordan is suspected to have a committed series of robberies in the local area. He was arrested and taken to the police station. What is one power of the police when arriving at the station?", "A": "Conduct a strip search", "B": "Detain him for 12 hours", "C": "Contact an appropriate adult", "D": "Take photographs and fingerprints", "Code": "C77", "Answer": "D"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Investigation Process", "Question": "What legal mechanism regulates police investigations and arrests in New South Wales (NSW)", "A": "The Police Investigation Commission (NSW)", "B": "The Australian Federal Police Act 1979 (Cwlth)", "C": "The Law Enforcement Conduct Commission Act 2016 (NSW)", "D": "The Law Enforcement (Powers and Responsibilities) Act 2002 (NSW) (LEPRA)", "Code": "C78", "Answer": "D"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Investigation Process", "Question": "Which of the following elements of the criminal investigation process is the correct order (first to last)?", "A": "Arrest, gathering evidence, bail, charge", "B": "Reporting, gathering evidence, charge, bail", "C": "Charge, arrest, gathering evidence, bail", "D": "Search, interrogation, bail, arrest", "Code": "C79", "Answer": "B"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Investigation Process", "Question": "What is the first step in the criminal investigation process?", "A": "Investigation of the crime", "B": "Arresting the accused person(s)", "C": "Reporting of the crime", "D": "Interrogation of the accused", "Code": "C80", "Answer": "C"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Investigation Process", "Question": "A phone app is released to make it easier for citizens to report suspected crimes to the police. How could this improve the criminal justice system?", "A": "Citizens could contribute to the criminal investigation process.", "B": "Citizens would be obliged to report all suspected activity.", "C": "Once a citizens report was made, police would have to investigate the matter.", "D": "Without a citizen report, police would not be able to use their investigative powers.", "Code": "C81", "Answer": "A"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Investigation Process", "Question": "Why is the reporting of crime an important part of the criminal investigation process?", "A": "More crimes are reported than unreported.", "B": "An unreported crime may not be investigated.", "C": "It is the only way police know that a crime has been committed.", "D": "It helps police to know how many crimes are occurring in a particular area.", "Code": "C82", "Answer": "B"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Investigation Process", "Question": "Under the Evidence Act (NSW) 1995, evidence that has been contaminated or compromised is known as:", "A": "Hearsay evidence", "B": "Inadmissible evidence", "C": "Admissible evidence", "D": "Circumstantial evidence", "Code": "C83", "Answer": "B"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Investigation Process", "Question": "Evidence is seized during a police search of a property. Which of the following statements is true?", "A": "The evidence is admissible if the police had a warrant.", "B": "Police do not have the power to seize evidence from properties.", "C": "The evidence is admissible if it is in the public interest to seize it.", "D": "The evidence is only admissible if the suspect is present during the search.", "Code": "C84", "Answer": "A"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Investigation Process", "Question": "What is the role of a magistrate during a committal hearing?", "A": "To question witnesses", "B": "To rule of points of law", "C": "To determine admissibility of evidence", "D": "To determine if there is a case to answer", "Code": "C85", "Answer": "D"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Investigation Process", "Question": "Which of the following describes the role of the jury in a criminal trial?", "A": "To make findings of fact", "B": "To provide unsworn evidence", "C": "To determine which evidence is admissible", "D": "To cross examine the victim on the victim impact statement", "Code": "C86", "Answer": "A"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Investigation Process", "Question": "Under the Evidence Act (NSW) 1995, what is the most important rule governing the admissibility of evidence?", "A": "It is scientific.", "B": "It is circumstantial.", "C": "It is relevant.", "D": "It is provided by the police.", "Code": "C87", "Answer": "C"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Investigation Process", "Question": "Corey is stopped by police whilst walking down the street. They ask to search his backpack and he complies. This can best be described as:", "A": "Illegal, as a drug detection dog was not used", "B": "Legal, as Corey consented to the search", "C": "Legal, as the police have unlimited search and seizure powers", "D": "Illegal, even though the police have told Corey the reason for the search", "Code": "C88", "Answer": "B"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Investigation Process", "Question": "A police officer has reason to suspect that Jordan has a pair of stolen sunglasses in his backpack. Which of the following statements is correct?", "A": "The police officer must arrest Jordan.", "B": "The police officer may search Jordan’s backpack.", "C": "Jordan will be required to attend the police station for questioning.", "D": "The police officer will require a warrant to search Jordan’s backpack.", "Code": "C89", "Answer": "B"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Investigation Process", "Question": "The suspect Samantha of carrying a carving knife which was used in an attack. What powers of search and seizure do the police have?", "A": "Search Samantha and confiscate the knife if they have a warrant", "B": "Only search Samantha and confiscate the knife if they arrest her", "C": "Confiscate the knife but not search Samantha without arresting her", "D": "Search Samantha without a warrant and confiscate the knife", "Code": "C90", "Answer": "D"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Investigation Process", "Question": "The ability of police to make a decision about whether to search an individual relates to which of the following concepts?", "A": "Discretion", "B": "Abuse of power", "C": "Rehabilitation", "D": "Profiling", "Code": "C91", "Answer": "A"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Investigation Process", "Question": "Police receive information that a suspect’s house is being used to manufacture large quantities of drugs for sale interstate. In which of the following situations can police search the suspects house?", "A": "A warrant has been granted.", "B": "A summons has been served.", "C": "The prosecutor authorises a search.", "D": "The Public Defender authorises a search.", "Code": "C92", "Answer": "A"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Investigation Process", "Question": "Which of the following describes a situation where police require a warrant?", "A": "To question a suspect", "B": "To record a phone conversation", "C": "To search a car suspected of containing illegal drugs", "D": "To pull over a person suspected of driving a stolen car", "Code": "C93", "Answer": "B"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Investigation Process", "Question": "Who would usually issue an arrest warrant?", "A": "The police?", "B": "The Premier", "C": "A judicial officer", "D": "The Attorney-General", "Code": "C94", "Answer": "C"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Investigation Process", "Question": "Alice has received an official legal document in relation to an offence she has allegedly committed. She is required to attend the local court on a certain date. What is this legal document known as?", "A": "An indictment", "B": "An injunction", "C": "A summons", "D": "A warrant", "Code": "C95", "Answer": "C"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Investigation Process", "Question": "When are police required to obtain a warrant?", "A": "Before they talk to a suspect", "B": "Before they talk to a witness", "C": "To search a motor vehicle that has a faulty light", "D": "To record a person’s mobile phone conversation", "Code": "C96", "Answer": "D"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Investigation Process", "Question": "Which of the following statements best describes a warrant?", "A": "An order by a court", "B": "A legal document requiring a person to attend court", "C": "An infringement notice informing a person they must pay a fine", "D": "A legal document outlining the evidence the police have gathered against an accused person", "Code": "C97", "Answer": "A"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Investigation Process", "Question": "Police receive information that a suspect’s house is being used to manufacture large quantities of drugs for sale interstate. In which of the following situations can police search the suspect’s house?", "A": "A summons has been served.", "B": "A warrant has been granted.", "C": "The prosecutor authorises a search.", "D": "The Public Defender authorises a search.", "Code": "C98", "Answer": "B"}, {"Syllabus point": "Criminal Investigation Process", "Question": "Spiro was approached by police while in a shopping centre. He verbally abuses an officer and is arrested. The police then travelled to Spiro’s friends house to search if there was anything unlawful there. Which of the following is correct?", "A": "The arrest is lawful; the search is lawful.", "B": "The arrest is lawful; the search is unlawful.", "C": "The arrest is unlawful; the search is unlawful.", "D": "The arrest is unlawful; the search is lawful.", "Code": "C99", "Answer": "B"}, {"Syllabus point": "Human Rights", "Question": "Which of the following statements best describes human rights protection in Australia?", "A": "A bill of rights offers significant protections.", "B": "Human rights are best protected in legislation.", "C": "Human rights are best protected under international law.", "D": "The United Nations is the only agency to enforce human rights.", "Code": "HR01", "Answer": "B"}, {"Syllabus point": "Human Rights", "Question": "A group of Indigenous people’s campaign for greater autonomy is an example of which of the following human rights?", "A": "Peace rights", "B": "Political rights", "C": "Self-determination"